\begin{align}
	\begin{split}
	&\ket{\Psi} = \Big( A \hat{c}^\dagger_0  + \sum_{\bm{k}} B_{\bm{k}} \hat{c}^\dagger_{-\bm{k}} \hat{\beta}^\dagger_{\bm{k}} +  \frac{1}{2} \sum_{\bm{k}\bm{p}} C_{\bm{k},\bm{p}} \hat{c}^\dagger_{-\bm{k}- \bm{p}} \hat{\beta}^\dagger_{\bm{k}} \hat{\beta}^\dagger_{\bm{p}} 
	\\&+ D \hat{d}^\dagger_{0} +
	\sum_{\bm{k}} E_{\bm{k}} \hat{d}^\dagger_{-\bm{k}} \hat{\beta}^\dagger_{\bm{k}} 
	\Big) \ket{\text{BEC}}.
	\label{eq:ansatz}
    \raisetag{25pt}
	\end{split}
\end{align}
The first three terms  describe the dressing of the impurity by $N=0,1,2$ Bogoliubov modes whereas the fourth and the fifth terms give the closed channel molecule components of the wave function including the dressing by up to $N=1$ Bogoliubov mode. 
Such a wave function has been used to successfully recover the experimental results 
regarding the spectral maximum of an impurity in a BEC~\cite{massignan2025polaronsatomicgasestwodimensional,Grusdt2024review}.
Here, 
we will also use the ansatz in Eq.~(\ref{eq:ansatz}) with time-dependent coefficients $A(t)$, $B_{\bm{k}}(t)$, $C_{\bm{k},\bm{p}}(t)$, $D(t)$, and $E_{\bm{k}}(t)$ to study quench dynamics~\cite{SM}.

For the ground state, the variational parameters in Eq.~(\ref{eq:ansatz}) can  be found by minimizing the expectation value  $\langle\Psi|\hat{H}|\Psi\rangle$ of the Hamiltonian. %One common caveat of this approach is that solving the resulting set of equations of motion is computationally expensive, and although finding the ground state is possible \cite{levinsen2015impurity}.
To overcome the challenge of accessing the excited states, we use the 
 Krylov subspace method~\cite{Yousef2003,Yousef2011,liesen2012krylov,nandy2024quantumdynamicskrylovspace}, which 
 %is particularly suited for systems where the  Hamiltonian matrix is difficult to store and treat with conventional diagonalization techniques. This method 
 iteratively constructs a reduced basis that captures the action of the Hamiltonian on the initial state, thereby forming a Krylov subspace~\cite{SM}. It offers efficient access to the low-energy spectrum, essential for the present study.

{\it Spectral function.}  The zero momentum impurity spectral function obtained from the Krylov subspace method is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:spectral_function} for the parameters $n_0^{1/3}a_- = -1$ and $n_0^{1/3}a_{\mathrm{B}} = 0.02$  allowing for a direct comparison
with Ref.~\cite{levinsen2015impurity}.  Near the unitary limit $1/k_n a=0$, the spectral function features two branches:
A sharp low-energy branch in agreement with the ground state of the problem studied in Ref.~\cite{levinsen2015impurity}, 
and a broad high-energy branch present at strong interactions. 
We furthermore see that the maximum in the spectral density of this branch is roughly in agreement with 
the ladder approximation equivalent to including terms with maximally one Bogoliubov mode in Eq.~\eqref{eq:ansatz}. 
This indicates that the upper branch  corresponds to states dressed by one boson, which decay to the  ground state dressed by two bosons within this approximation. 
Importantly, the spectral weight of the upper branch vastly exceeds that of the ground state branch  
suggesting that it is the primary contributor to most experimental observables.
We find that these qualitative features are robust for  $n_0^{1/3}|a_-|\gtrsim 1$. 


%This suggests that low-lying few-body states~\cite{levinsen2015impurity,shchadilovaQuantumDynamicsUltracold2016} may not be the primary contributors to the dynamics of the system near the unitary limit and for $1/k_n a > 0 $.



%The width of the broad peak, $\Gamma_{pol}$, depends on $|a_-|$, which defines decay channels available for the impurity in the BEC. In general, we observe a decrease of the spectral weight when increasing $|a_-|$~\cite{SM}. However, even if $n^{1/3}|a_-| \gg 1$, which is the case for polaron experiments with $^{39}$K, we still observe a broad feature~\cite{SM}.

%Near the unitary limit and for $1/k_n a > 0 $,  the spectral weight of the high-energy branch vastly exceeds that of the low-lying state. As a result, the high-energy branch dominates the system’s spectral response. 
%This suggests that low-lying few-body states~\cite{levinsen2015impurity,shchadilovaQuantumDynamicsUltracold2016} may not be the primary contributors to the dynamics of the system near the unitary limit and for $1/k_n a > 0 $.




