The remaining part of \Cref{thm: sbt} requires establishing a distributional limit for $(\log_2 h(\mathcal T(\pi_n) - n/2)/(\sqrt n /2) \to_d |Z|$ as $n \to \infty$ to $|Z|$ for $Z \sim N(0,1)$ for uniform simple butterfly permutations $\pi_n$. This follows using standard results from probability theory: %, such as Slutsky's theorem, the Central Limit Thoerem, noting $h(\mathcal T(\pi_n)) $






% \subsection{Distributional limit}

% Here's some scratch work to formalize the limit. Recall Slutsky's theorem, which states if $(U_n,V_n)$ is a random vector with $U_n \to_d U$ and $V_n \to_p c$ (a constant), then $U_n + V_n \to_d U + c$ and $U_n V_n \to_d cU$; my goal is to use this in a few ways. 

Let  $Z_n = 2(X_n - n/2)/\sqrt n$ with $Z_n \to_d Z\sim N(0,1)$ by the Central Limit Theorem (CLT), with $h_n^{\B},X_n$ defined as in \eqref{eq: h sb form}. Let $R_n = h_n^{\B} + 2$. Then $$W_n := 2^{-n/2} R_n = 2^{X_n - n/2} + 2^{n/2 - X_n} = 2 \cosh(\ln 2 \sqrt n |Z_n|/2)  = 2^{\sqrt n |Z_n|/2}(1 + 2^{-\sqrt n |Z_n|}),$$ and now let $$Q_n = W_n^{2/\sqrt n} := A_n B_n^{2/\sqrt n}$$ where $A_n = 2^{|Z_n|}$ and $B_n = 1 + 2^{-\sqrt n |Z_n|}$. Note first $B_n \to_p 1$. This is straight forward, but to include the details: Fix $\epsilon > 0$, and note $$\P(|B_n - 1| = 2^{-\sqrt n|Z_n|} > \epsilon) = \P\left(|Z_n| < \frac{\log_2 \frac1\epsilon}{\sqrt n}\right)$$
If $\epsilon \ge 1$, then this evaluates directly to 0 since $|Z_n| \ge 0$ while $\log_2 \frac1\epsilon \le 0$, so we only need to consider $\epsilon \in (0,1)$. Fix $\delta > 0$ and let $N_\delta$ be such that $\frac{\log_2 \frac1\epsilon}{ \sqrt n} < \delta$ if $n \ge N_\delta$. It follows then 
$$
\P(|B_n - 1| > \epsilon) \le \P(|Z_n| < \delta)
$$
for $n \ge N_\delta$, so that
$$
\limsup_{n \to \infty} \P(|B_n - 1| > \epsilon) \le \P(|Z| < \delta) = 2 \Phi(\delta) - 1 = o(\delta)
$$
by the CLT, with $\Phi$ denoting the cumulative distribution function for $Z$; it follows $B_n \to_p 1$ by taking $\delta \to 0$. In particular, then also $B_n^{2/\sqrt n} \to_p 1$ by other standard probabilistic methods. %\footnote{For the additional details: this is very straightforward, but can be explicitly outlined using the equivalence of convergence in probability and in distribution along with Slutsky's theorem to yield $B_n^{2/\sqrt n} = \exp(\frac2{\sqrt n} \ln B_n)$; the continuous mapping theorem yields $\ln B_n \to_d \ln 1 = 0$ while the sequence $\frac2{\sqrt n}\to_p 1$, so their product converges in distribution to 0 and hence also in probability, and so $B_n^{2/\sqrt n}\to_p\exp(0) = 1$ again by the Continuous mapping theorem.} 
Since $A_n \to_d 2^{|Z|}$ (by the Continuous mapping theorem), it follows then by Slutsky's theorem that $$Q_n = A_n B_n^{2/\sqrt n} \to_d 2^{|Z|},$$ where we recall $$Q_n = W_n^{2/\sqrt n} = (2^{-n/2} R_n)^{2/\sqrt n} = [2^{-n/2} (h_n^{\B}+2)]^{2/\sqrt n}.$$
Hence, also (by the Continuous mapping theorem) $$\log_2 Q_n = 2(\log_2 (h_n^{\B} + 2) - n/2)/\sqrt n \to_d |Z|.$$
Replacing $h_n^{\B} + 2$ directly with just $h_n^{\B}$ above then has the same distributional limit (via Slutsky's theorem), i.e., 
$$
2(\log_2 h_n^{\B} - n/2)/\sqrt n \to_d |Z|.
$$
(This mirrors the same normalization that determined $Z_n$ from $X_n$ for the CLT application above.) Equivalently, this shows
$$
[2^{-n/2} h_n^{\B}]^{2/\sqrt n} = [h_n^{\B}/ \sqrt N]^{2/\sqrt n} = [(h_n^{\B})^2/N]^{1/\sqrt n} \to_d 2^{|Z|}.
$$
%Equivalently, $[(h_n^{\B})^2/ N]^{1/(\sqrt n \ln 2)} \to_d e^{|Z|}$.
Combining all of these results now completes the proof of \Cref{thm: sbt}.

\subsection{Nonsimple butterfly trees: \texorpdfstring{\boldmath$\mathcal{T}^{\B}_n$}{TnB}}\label{sec: nonsimple}

Now we turn to nonsimple butterfly trees, $\mathcal T_n^{\B}$, which are generated using $\boldsymbol \pi \sim \Unif(\B_n)$. Unlike the simple case, where the generating simple permutations are constructed using Kronecker products, the nonsimple case involves wreath product structures. For instance, instead of structures like $12 \otimes \pi_1 = \pi_1 \oplus \pi_1$ or $21 \otimes \pi_1 = \pi_1 \ominus \pi_1$, the nonsimple setting allows for different subblocks, such as $\pi_1 \oplus \pi_2$ and $\pi_1 \ominus \pi_2$, with $\pi_1, \pi_2$ not necessarily equal. These can be alternatively written as: $\pi_1 \oplus \pi_2 = (12 \otimes \pi_1)(1 \oplus \pi_1^{-1} \pi_2)$. This greater structural flexibility complicates the analysis of the height $h_n^{\B} = h(\mathcal T(\boldsymbol \pi))$ for $\boldsymbol \pi \sim \Unif(\B_n)$, making a full distributional characterization (cf. \Cref{thm: sbt}) less tractable. Instead, we focus on bounding the expected height. We establish power-law bounds so that $cN^{\alpha}\cdot (1+o(1))\leq E[h_n^B]\leq dN^{\beta}\cdot (1+o(1))$ for $\beta \approx 0.913189$, as detailed in \Cref{thm: nsbt}.

For a permutation of these block structures, %\footnote{This can be generalized to consider permutations of the form $\pi = (\rho \otimes \operatorname{id_n})\cdot \bigoplus_{j = 1}^m \pi_k$ with $\rho \in S_m$ and $\pi_k \in S_n$ so that $\pi \in S_{nm}$.}, 
these have corresponding BST $\mathcal T(\pi)$ that can similarly be constructed recursively by replacing each node of an external BST with an internal BST at each generating step. %Generalizing the simple binary butterfly permutations, we will now consider the nonsimple binary butterfly permutations, which are constructed analogously to how $B_{n,s} = \bigotimes_{j=1}^n S_2$ was formed but now allowing different sub-blocks at each block node location. Noting the conjugation action of $21 \otimes \operatorname{id}_{N/2}$ on the permutation $\pi_1 \oplus \pi_2$ returns $\pi_2 \oplus \pi_1$, that satisfies a wreath product action, then we have the nonsimple (binary) butterfly permutations satisfy $B_1 = 1$ and 
% $$
% B_{N} = (B_{N/2} \oplus B_{N/2}) \rtimes \langle 21 \otimes \operatorname{id}_{N/2}\rangle \cong S_2 \wr S_2 \wr \cdots \wr S_2
% $$
% is an $n$-iterated wreath product of $S_2 \cong C_2$ (for $C_k$ the cyclic group of order $k$). Since $B_{2^n}$ is a subgroup of $S_{2^n}$, where $|B_{2^n}| = 2^{2^n - 1}$ while $2^n! = 2^{2^n-1} m$ for odd $m$, then $B_{2^n}$ necessarily comprises a $2$-Sylow subgroup of $S_{2^n}$.
