To analyze heights for these corresponding butterfly trees, we will leverage the recursive construction and relationships between each corresponding level of permutations. Let $\boldsymbol \pi \in B_{n+1}$, and so let $\rho \in S_2$ and $\pi_1,\pi_2 \in B_{n}$ such that $\boldsymbol \pi= (\rho \otimes \operatorname{id})(\pi_1 \oplus \pi_2)$. Let $H_{n+1} = h(\mathcal T(\boldsymbol \pi))$, $H_n = h(\mathcal T(\pi_1))$, $H_n' = h(\mathcal T(\pi_2))$, $L_{n+1} = \ell(\mathcal T(\boldsymbol \pi))$, $L_n = \ell(\mathcal T(\pi_1))$, $L_n' = \ell(\mathcal T(\pi_2))$, $R_{n+1} = r(\mathcal T(\boldsymbol \pi))$, $R_n = r(\mathcal T(\pi_1))$, and $R_n' = r(\mathcal T(\pi_2))$. \Cref{fig: ns bin tree} shows the two structures for $\mathcal T(\boldsymbol \pi)$ depending on the values of $\rho \in S_2$. The corresponding values for $\boldsymbol \pi$ depend on $\rho \in S_2$ along with the corresponding values for $\pi_1$ and $\pi_2$. For example, for $\boldsymbol \pi = \pi_1 \ominus \pi_2$ (as in \Cref{fig: ns bin tree}b, where $\rho = 21$) then $R_{n+1} = R_n'$ and $L_{n+1} = L_n' + 1 + L_n$, while $H_{n+1} = \max(L_n' + 1 + H_n,H_n')$. \Cref{fig: ns bin tree} shows an instance where $H_{n+1} = H_n' = 7 > L_n' + 1 + H_n = 0 + 1 + 4 = 5$; note for further comparison, the included $\boldsymbol \pi = \pi_1 \ominus \pi_2$ has $\operatorname{LIS}(\pi) = 8$ while $\operatorname{LDS}(\pi) = 5$. In general, then $(H_n,L_n,R_n)$ satisfy the following recursion formulae:
\begin{align}
    L_{n+1} &= \left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        L_n, & \rho = 12\\
        L_n' + 1 + L_n, \hspace{3.9pc}& \rho = 21
    \end{array}
    \right. \label{eq: Ln ns rec}\\
    R_{n+1} &= \left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        R_n + 1 + R_n', \hspace{3.8pc}& \rho = 12\\
        R_n', & \rho = 21
    \end{array}
    \right.\label{eq: Rn ns rec}\\
    H_{n+1} &= \left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        \max(R_n + 1 + H_n',H_n), & \rho = 12\\
        \max(L_n' + 1 + H_n, H_n'), & \rho = 21
    \end{array}
    \right. \label{eq: Hn ns rec}
\end{align}
From \eqref{eq: Hn ns rec}, it's clear $(H_n,L_n,R_n)$ cannot satisfy a simple recursion as seen for simple binary butterfly trees (cf. \eqref{eq: Hn s rec}). 

The appearance of the $\max$ in the heights is the main obstacle in computing the first moment. However, the recursive formulae for each $L_n$ and $R_n$ allow direct analysis, which also compares directly to the number of cycles of a nonsimple (binary) butterfly permutation, $C(\pi_n) \sim Y_n$ where $Y_0 = 1$ and $Y_{n+1} \sim Y_n + \eta_n Y_n'$ for $Y_n \sim Y_n'$ iid, independent of $\eta_n =\mathds 1(\rho = 12) \sim \Bern(1/2)$, as shown previously in \Cref{thm: old bsbt}. Now \eqref{eq: Ln ns rec} and \eqref{eq: Rn ns rec} yield $L_n$ and $R_n$ satisfy the same recurrence (up to shifting). Moreover, noting $C(\boldsymbol{\pi}) = C(\boldsymbol{\pi_2}) + \mathds 1(\rho = 12) \cdot C(\boldsymbol{\pi_1})$ (see \cite{PZ24}), we have directly from induction that $C(\boldsymbol{\pi}) = r(\mathcal T(\boldsymbol{\pi})) + 1$. We summarize this as:
\begin{lemma}
    Let $\boldsymbol \pi \sim \Unif(\B_n)$. Then $C(\boldsymbol \pi) = r(\mathcal T(\boldsymbol \pi))+1 \sim \ell(\mathcal T(\boldsymbol \pi)) + 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{remark}
    In both the simple and nonsimple cases, different structural features of the permutation correspond directly to the same geometric quantity in the associated butterfly tree. For simple butterfly permutations—constructed via Kronecker products—the right edge length satisfies $r(\mathcal T(\pi)) + 1 = \lds(\pi)$, linking it to the length of the longest decreasing subsequence (cf. \Cref{l: simple lis r}). In contrast, for nonsimple butterfly permutations—formed using wreath products—we find that now $r(\mathcal T(\pi)) + 1 = C(\pi)$, aligning the right edge instead with the number of cycles.
\end{remark}



