\end{remark}


\begin{remark}
    Experiments suggest that the average height of nonsimple butterfly trees lies much closer to the lower bound in \Cref{thm: nsbt}. For example, computing the heights of 10\,000 such trees with $N = 2^{10} = 1\,024$ nodes—each built from an iid uniform nonsimple butterfly permutation  (of which there are $2^{N-1} \approx 8.99 \cdot 10^{307}$)—yields a sample mean of $119.15$. This is much nearer to the theoretical lower bound of $113.33$ than to the upper bound of $1\,313.53$ (which exceeds the maximal possible height of $N-1=1\,023$). \Cref{fig:sample bsts histogram} shows a histogram of the computed heights from these 10\,000 trials. While the sample minimum of $62$ matched the exact minimal height for the simple butterfly trees (cf.\ \Cref{t: s height cnts}), the sample maximum of $242$ was smaller than that of roughly 11\% ($112/1\,024$) of the simple butterfly trees.
\end{remark}



