We now consider the heights of the corresponding butterfly trees. We consider first the case for $m = 3$, whose main building blocks are shown in \Cref{fig: tern BST}. Let $H_n = h(\mathcal T(\pi_n))$, $L_n = h_L(\mathcal T(\pi_n))$, $h_R(\mathcal T(\pi_n))$, and similarly for $H_{n+1},L_{n+1},R_{n+1}$ using $\rho \otimes \pi_n$ where $\rho \in B_{s,3}$. Using \Cref{fig: tern BST}, we can establish the necessary recurrence by replacing each node in $\mathcal T(\rho)$ with copies of (appropriately label-adjusted) $\mathcal T(\pi)$, which yield:
\begin{align*}
    H_{n+1} &= H_n + \left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        2R_n + 2, & \rho = 123\\
        \max(L_n,R_n) + 1, & \rho = 231\\
        L_n +R_n + 2, & \rho = 312
    \end{array}
    \right.\\
    L_{n+1} &= \left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        L_n, & \rho = 123\\
        2L_n + 1, & \rho = 231 \ \mbox{or} \  312%\\
        % H_n + L_n +R_n + 2, & \rho = 312
    \end{array}
    \right.\\
    R_{n+1} &= \left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        3R_n + 2, & \rho = 123\\
        2R_n + 1, & \rho = 231\\
        R_n, & \rho = 312
    \end{array}
    \right.
\end{align*}
Both $L_n$ and $R_n$ can be described  directly: when $\pi_n \in B_{s,3^n}^{(3)}$, then it follows $$\log_2(L_{n+1} + 1) = \mathds 1(\rho \ne 123) + \log_2(L_n+1),$$ so that inductively  $L_n + 1 = \operatorname{LDS}(\pi_n) \sim \operatorname{Binom}(n,1 - \frac13)$. Similarly, it's clear
$$
R_n+1 = \prod_{j=1}^n X_j, \qquad X_j \sim \operatorname{Unif}(\{1,2,3\}).
$$
Hence, it is straightforward on how to deal with each $L_n, R_n$ asymptotically as well as for fixed $n$. For instance, noting if $\log_2 X \sim \operatorname{Binom}(n,1-p)$ then $\E X^{\alpha} =(2^\alpha (1-p) + p)^n$, so that $\E L_n = (\frac53)^n - 1$ while $\E R_n = 2^n -1$. Note $\E |L_n - R_n| \ge |\E L_n - \E R_n| = 2^n-(\frac53)^n$ while also $\E |L_n - R_n| \le \E L_n + \E R_n = 2^n + (\frac53)^n  - 2$. It follows $\E |L_n - R_n| = \E R_n \cdot (1 + o(1))$, so that $\E \max(L_n,R_n) = \E R_n \cdot (1 + o(1))$ and hence
$$
\E H_{n+1} = \E H_n + \frac43 2^n \cdot (1 + o(1)),
$$
which yields $$\E H_n = \frac43 (2^n - 1) \cdot (1 + o(1)) = \frac43 N^\gamma \cdot (1 + o(1)), \qquad \gamma = \log_3(2) \approx 0.6309.$$



