\section{Other ideas, potential paper structure?}

Given the other feedback, it would be good to reassess how to frame this project. I think it might be better to frame this in first in line with what Diaconis/Borodin had looked at by considering ``colored permutations'' and general wreath products of two permutations. (We could also reach out for other feedback from people. Would Persi be worth asking?) Maybe we can get a more general result of the height of $S_n \wr S_m$ where both $n,m$ grow (likely considering cutoffs as seen in the Diaconis paper, where $m = n/\log n$ or something like that). I would be interested in particular if then there is still a LLN for this setup (would it limit in probability to $(c^*)^2$ for $c^*\approx 4.311$ when scaling by $\log n \cdot \log m$?) In particular, by gluing together BSTs by either necessarily traversing the top right or top left edge (i.e., the path from the root to either 1 or $|V|$) then I think we would need to have asymptotics on $R_n$, $L_n$ as well for the uniform model, rather than just $H_n$. These are the first monotonic sequence within the permutations that connects the first entry to either 1 or $|V|$. So these are dominated by the LDS/LIS trivially (but that's not surprising -- typically, the LDS/LIS don't materialize within the BST using the binary comparison structure -- one example can use I looked at uses the permutation $\pi = [6,3,16,11,7,17,14,8,5,19,15,1,2,4,18,13,9,20,10,12] \in S_{20}$. The tableau is in 1,2,4,9,10,12;3,5,8,13,18,20;6,7,14,15;11,17,19;16. So this has LIS = 6 (it happens the top row is also an LIS), LDS = 5 (e.g., 16,14,8,5,1). The height = 6 can be attained through the BST path connecting 6,16,11,7,8,9,10 (so this traversal goes RLLRRR). It might be interesting to form a BST for an even larger permutation, because then marking the LIS path within the BST makes it more apparent how the LIS can dominate the height -- because the height is a down only path in the BST, with the LIS and LDS as seen within the BST allows up and lateral movements.) For instance, here's a random permutation in $S_{30}$: 12,20,2,14,15,10,25,13,18,22,8,21,27,26,28,9,1,5,17,11,29,7,30,6,23,24,16,3,4,19. Just using my available functions, this has height 6, lis = 9, lds = 8. Making the BST, the height can achieved through 4 paths, as nodes 4,6,16,20 all have depth 6. So the right edge happens (shortest monotonic sequence from 12 to 30, that goes 12-20-25-27-28-29-30) to match the max depth, while the left edge is length 2 (from the shortest monotonic sequence from 12 to 1, which goes 12-2-1). (Note a LIS goes 12-14-15-18-22-26-28-29-30 (compare to the right edge path) while a LDS goes 25-22-21-17-11-6-3). In particular, a quick take away is why the height would be dominated asymptotically by the LIS (and LDS) by viewing a corresponding such sequence within the BST model -- the height is only achievable by traveling through the BST using downward paths, while the LIS and LDS can also use upperward movements along with moving outside of the BST, so it is much less restricted in how much it can grow.



So I think we can come up with a rule for the law of the top right/top left lengths within the random BST, that is more interesting in general. For $\pi \in S_n$, then $\pi(1) \sim \operatorname{Unif}([n])$. If $\pi(1) = 1$, then $h_L(\mathcal T(\pi)) = 0$. Otherwise, we now consider the subsequence $\rho \subset \pi$ of indices formed by iteratively keeping the minimal value of the first $k$ entries, which corresponds to $\tilde \rho \in S_{k}$ for $k = \pi(1) - 1$, which is uniform in $S_k$ when conditioning on $\pi(1)$. Then repeat, with then $h_L(\mathcal T(\pi)) = 1 + h_L(\mathcal T(\tilde \rho))$. So the left edge length is the stopping time for this algorithm (and analogously this models the right edge length). So, this should be pretty straightforward to analyze. When $\pi \sim \Unif(S_n)$, let $\ell_n = h_L(\mathcal T(\pi))$. Set $\ell_1= 0$. Then $\ell_n = 0$ if $\pi(1) = 1$ and $\ell_n = h_L(\mathcal T(\tilde \rho)) + 1$ if $\pi(1) > 1$; in particular, then $\ell_n \sim \ell_{n-k+1} + 1$ if $\pi(1) = k$ for $k > 1$. Hence, $$\E \ell_n = \sum_{k = 2}^{n} \E(\ell_{n-k+1} + 1) \P(\pi(1) = n-k+1) = \frac1n \sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} (\E \ell_k + 1) = 1 - \frac1n + \frac1n \sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} \E \ell_k.$$
Without the $-\frac1n$ term in the summand has the recurrence solution $H_n = \sum_{j=1}^n \frac1j$. (Here $H_n$ is used for harmonic numbers -- so the notation earlier should be adjusted to accommodate the more standard use of $H_n$ as these are now potentially both present.) This wasn't an obvious recurrence for Harmonic numbers for me (but maybe it should have been?), but it does work out after checking on this. I checked by hand the first few terms (up to $H_4 = \frac{25}{12}$) and they all worked. So in general, a simple check verifies $H_1 = 1$ while also $$1 + \frac1n\sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} H_k = 1 + \frac1n \sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} \sum_{j=1}^k \frac1j = 1 + \frac1n \sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} \frac1k \cdot (n-k) = 1 + \sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} \left(\frac1k - \frac1n\right) =  H_{n-1} + \frac1n = H_n$$
Hence, we have $\E \ell_n \le H_n$. Okay, but it's actually (again, not obviously to me -- this was after a bunch more computations) true that $\E \ell_n = H_n - 1$ for $n \ge 2$. For this, I can leverage the previous recurrence for Harmonic numbers; using induction to prove $\E \ell_n = H_n - 1$, clearly $\E \ell_1 = 0 = H_1 - 1$, while then assuming the result holds for $k < n$ we then have
$$
\E \ell_n = 1 - \frac1n + \frac1n \sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} \E \ell_k = 1 - \frac1n + \frac1n \sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} (H_k - 1) = \left[ 1 + \frac1n\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} H_k\right] - \frac1n\left[1 + n-1 \right] = H_n - 1.
$$

Next, we can compute the second moment:
\begin{align*}
    \E \ell_n^2 &= \frac1n\sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} \E[(\ell_{k} + 1)^2] = \frac1n \sum_{k = 1}^{n-1}\E[ \ell_k^2 + 2 \ell_k + 1] = \frac1n \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} [\E \ell_k^2 -1] + 2 \cdot \frac1n \sum_{k=1}^{n-1}(\E \ell_k + 1) \\
    &= \frac1n \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \E \ell_k^2 + 2\E \ell_n - \frac{n-1}n = 2 H_n + \frac1n - 3 + \frac1n \sum_{k = 1}^{n-1} \E \ell_k^2
\end{align*}
This can be computed by defining $S_n = \sum_{k = 1}^n \E \ell_k^2$ with $S_1 =0$, so that
$$
S_n = \left(1 + \frac1n\right)S_{n-1} + 2 H_n + \frac1n - 3
$$
and hence (noting $\prod_{k = j+1}^n (1 + \frac1k) = \frac{n+1}{j+1}$ while $\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} 2H_j/(j+1) = H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)}$ where $H^{(k)}_n = \sum_{j=1}^n 1/j^k$, while also noting the $j = 1$ term evaluates to 0 so the summand index can be expanded)
\begin{align*}
    S_n &= \sum_{j = 2}^n \left(2 H_j + \frac1j - 3\right)\prod_{k = j+1}^n \left(1 + \frac1k\right)=(n+1) \sum_{j = 1}^n \left[\frac{2 H_j}{j+1} + \frac1{j(j+1)} - \frac3{j+1}\right]\\
    &=(n+1)\left[\left(H_{n+1}^2 - H_{n+1}^{(2)}\right) + \left(1 - \frac1{n+1}\right) - 3(H_{n+1} - 1)\right].
\end{align*}
It follows 
\begin{align*}
    \E L_n^2 &= 2H_n + \frac1n - 3 + \frac{S_{n-1}}n = 2 H_n + \frac1n - 3 + \left[\left(H_{n}^2 - H_{n}^{(2)}\right) + \left(1 - \frac1{n}\right) - 3(H_{n} - 1)\right]\\
    &= H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)} - H_n + 1,
\end{align*}
and hence (noting $H_n = \log n + \gamma + o(1)$ and $H_n^{(2)} = \frac{\pi^2}6 + o(1)$)
\begin{align*}
    \Var(L_n) &= \E L_n^2 - (\E L_n^2) = (H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)} - H_n + 1) - (H_n - 1)^2 = H_n - H_n^{(2)} = \log n \cdot (1 + o(1)).
\end{align*}
It follows then $\Var(L_n/\log n) = O(1/\log n) = o(1)$ while also $\E L_n/\log n = 1 + o(1)$ so that $$L_n/\log n \to_p 1.$$
I'm interested then if it's straightforward to potentially now derive a CLT for $L_n$, where perhaps $Z_n = (L_n - \log n)/\sqrt{\log n}$ converges in distribution to $Z \sim N(0,1)$ -- perhaps coupling $L_n$ to an independent Stirling distribution $\Upsilon_n$, with $\P(\Upsilon_n = k) = |s(n,k)|/n!$ that satisfies $\E \Upsilon_k = H_n = \E L_n +1$ and $\Var \Upsilon_n = H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)} = \Var L_n $, especially as this normalization for $Z_n$ applied to $\Upsilon_n$ does have a standard normal distributional limit. (How many more moments would be needed? This might be more directly primed for analysis using mod-$\phi$ convergence methods.) In particular, is there then a direct connection between the first decreasing subsequence of $\pi$ (i.e., the depth of the node 1 in the associated BST (minus 1)) and the number of cycles for any permutation? Has this been established or even conjectured before? (I'm guessing there's also something more subtle here, as $L_n \sim R_n$ -- and these are negatively correlated, but they can't both point to the number of cycles.) Okay, actually we can't have $L_n + 1$ equal in distribution, as repeating the above recursive model, we can compute the pmf via recursion as well: let $p_n(k) = \P(L_n = k-1)$. We have $p_n(1) = \P(\pi(1) = 1) = \frac1n$ while conditioning on $\pi(1)$, it follows $p_n(2) = \frac1n \sum_{j = 1}^{n-1} p_j(1) = H_{n-1}/n$, and similarly $p_n(3) = \frac1n \sum_{j = 2}^{n-1} p_j(2) = \frac1n \sum_{j = 1}^{n-2} H_j/(j+1) = (H_{n-1}^2 - H_{n-1}^{(2)})/(2n)$. In general, 
