\section{Newer stuff}

The last section is more scratch work, but here are some of the main takeaways.

We are first interested in studying the heights of $\pi \sim \Unif(S_n \wr S_m)$. We can outline how block permutations work, and behave with BSTs -- as the outside BST for the $m$-permutations yields the main skeleton of the resulting BST, where the nodes are replaced by BSTs of the $n$-permutations that have the $m$-BST edges gluing together these $n$-BSTs via either the top left or top right edges of the BSTs. So do understand the heights of the wreath product BST, we first need to establish more about the distribution of the lengths of the top edges. Let $L_n,R_n$ be these respect lengths of the resulting BST for $\pi \sim \Unif(S_n)$. Clearly $L_n \sim R_n$ as $\pi \mapsto n - p + 1$ yields a bijection for sets with $L_n = k$ to $R_n = k$. What was less clear, but is now pretty crystal clear, is that $L_n\sim \Upsilon_n - 1$, where $\P(\Upsilon_n = k) = |s(n,k)|/n!$ denotes the Stirling-1 distribution (with $s(n,k)$ the Stirling numbers of the first kind), that traditionally modeled the distribution of the number of cycles of a uniform permutation. To see this, let $l(n,k) = n! \cdot \P(L_n = k-1 = \#\{\pi \in S_n: h_L(\pi) = k - 1\}$. Clearly $l(n,1) = (n-1)!$ as these correspond to the permutations where $\pi(1) = 1$, so clearly then $l(n,1) = |s(n,1)|$ as this is also the number of $n$-cycles in $S_n$. Note $|s(n+1,k)| = n|s(n,k)| + |s(n,k-1)|$ is the standard recursion that determines higher order Stirling numbers, so it suffices to now establish $l(n,k)$ also satisfies this recursion. This is clear by considering how to generate a permutation in $S_{n+1}$ by placing $n+1$ inside a permutation $\pi \in S_n$. There are $n+1$ spaces to place this $n+1$; so we will consider ways to do this that lead of the top left edge have length $k-1$. If $n+1$ is placed in the first entry, then the resulting permutation has BST that has root $n+1$, and so the left subtree of this root matches exactly the BST from $\pi$, and so we have $l(n,k-1)$ such permutations $\pi \in S_n$ that together yields a permutation in $S_{n+1}$ with top left length $k-1$. If $n+1$ is entered anywhere else, then this follows an insert into the BST for $\pi$, that only adds on an extra node at the end of the top right edge of the $pi$ BST and so doesn't impact the length of the top left edge -- since there are $n$ spaces left to choose, and each can have $l(n,k)$ options of $\pi$ in $S_n$ with the desired top-left edge length, then together this yields $n l(n,k)$ such options. Hence, we have $l(n+1,k) = n l(n,k) + l(n,k-1)$, as so from induction we thus have $|s(n,k)| = l(n,k)$ for all $n$.

This is useful because $\E \Upsilon_n = H_n$ and $\Var \Upsilon_n = H_n - H_n^{(2)}$ together yield $\Upsilon_n /\log n \to_p 1$ (since $H_n = \log n \cdot (1 + o(1))$). Moreover, we also have for free the CLT result $(L_n - \log n)/\sqrt{\log n} \to_d N(0,1)$ (from Goncharov, but this is also very clear from the Quasi power theorem of Hwang, since $\E u^{\Upsilon_n} = u(u+1) \cdot (u + n-1)/n! = \frac{\Gamma(u + n)}{\Gamma(u) n!} = A(u) B(u)^{\lambda_n} \cdot (1 + O(1/\varphi_n))$ where $A(u) = 1/\Gamma(u)$ and $B(u) = e^{u-1}$ are analytic in a neighborhood of 1 (using the Stirling approximation for $\Gamma(u + n)$), where $B(u) = B'(u) = B''(u)$, and $\lambda_n = \log n$ and $\varphi_n = n$ are both $\omega(1)$, and moreover $\mu = B'(1) = 1$ and $\sigma^2 = B''(1) + B'(1) - B'(1)^2 = 1$ are such that $\E \Upsilon_n = \mu \lambda_n + O(1 + \lambda_n/\varphi_n)$ and $\Var \Upsilon_n = \sigma^2 \lambda_n + O(1 + \lambda_n/\varphi_n)$, which then yields $(\Upsilon_n - \E \Upsilon_n)/\sqrt{\Var \Upsilon_n} \to_d N(0,1)$). 

Now for $\pi \sim \Unif(S_n \wr S_m)$, write $h_{n,m} = h(\mathcal T(\pi))$, and $h_k = h_{k,1}$. So this generalizes Devroye's result by allowing multiple layers. If $\pi = (\mathbf{\pi})(\rho \otimes \operatorname{id}_n) \in S_n \wr S_m$ for $\mathbf \pi \in S_n^m$ and $\rho \in S_m$, then we have a lower bound on $h(\mathcal T(\pi))$ by considering a vertex $v \in \pi_v \in S_n$ (that is, a label within a particular $n$-permutation BST I am thus calling $\pi_v$) of maximal depth within a maximal depth node from $\rho$ (since the height then maximizes over all such nodes; all vertices within the same $\rho$-node block share the same BST paths from above connecting the root of $\pi$ to the root of the particular $m$-node; so there are $h(\mathcal T(\rho))$ block nodes that are traversed to get to the particular node, and each is traversed either along the top left or top right edge -- it follows $\operatorname{depth}(v) = h(\mathcal T(\pi_v)) + \sum_{j = 1}^{h(\mathcal T(\rho))}X_{n,j}$ where $X_{n,j} \sim Upsilon_n \sim L_n + 1$. Hence, we have
$$
h_{m,n} \ge Z_{n,m} := h_n + \sum_{j=1}^{h_m}X_{n,j},
$$
where $h_n$ is independent of $X_{n,j} \sim \Upsilon_n$ iid, that are each independent of $h_m$. If $m$ is fixed, then $h_n/\log n \to_p c^*$ while $X_{n,j}/\log n \to_p 1$ together yield (via Slutsky and using the equivalence of limits in distribution and probability to constants, and noting also necessarily $\lfloor \log_2 m\rfloor \le h_m \le m-1$)
\begin{align*}
    \lim_{n \to \infty}\P\left(\sum_{j=1}^{h_m} X_{n,j} \le t\log n\right) &= \lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{k = \lfloor \log_2 m \rfloor}^{m-1} \P\left(\left.\sum_{j = 1}^k X_{n,j} \le t\log n \right| h_m = k\right)\P(h_m = k) \\
    &= \sum_{k = \lfloor \log_2 m \rfloor}^{m-1} \lim_{n \to \infty} \P\left(\sum_{j = 1}^kX_{n,j} \le t\log n\right)\P(h_m = k) \\
    &= \sum_{k = \lfloor \log_2 m \rfloor}^{m-1} \P\left(\sum_{j = 1}^k 1 \le t\right)\P(h_m = k) \\
    &= \sum_{k = \lfloor \log_2 m \rfloor}^{m-1} \P(k \le t) \P(h_m = k)\\
    &= \sum_{k = \lfloor \log_2 m \rfloor}^{m-1} \P(h_m \le t,h_m = k)\\
    &=\P(h_m \le t)
\end{align*}
so that $Z_{m,n}/\log n \to_d c^* + h_m$ (for $m$ fixed). If $n$ is fixed, now we work with $W_m = \sum_{j = 1}^{h_m} X_{n,j}/h_m = \overline {(\Upsilon_n)}_{h_m}$ (a sample mean with $h_m$ iid samples of $\Upsilon_n$). Then $Z_{n,m} = h_n + h_m W_m$. If $m \to\infty$, then $h_n/\log m \to_p 0$, $h_m/ \log m \to_p c^*$, and now I claim $W_m \to_p \E \Upsilon_n = H_n$; together, this would then yield $Z_{n,m}/\log m \to_p c^* H_n$ (when $n$ is fixed). Now to see $W_m \to_p H_n$, we make use of the SLLN that $$\P\left(\lim_{d \to \infty} \overline{(\Upsilon_n)_d} = H_n\right) = \P\left(\bigcap_{q \ge 1} \bigcup_{r \ge 1} \bigcap_{s \ge r} \left\{\left|\overline{(\Upsilon_n)_s} - H_n\right|<\frac1q\right\} \right) = 1,$$ and equivalently
$$\P\left(\lim_{d \to \infty} \overline{(\Upsilon_n)_d} \ne H_n\right) = \P\left(\bigcup_{q \ge 1} \bigcap_{r \ge 1} \bigcup_{s \ge r} \left\{\left|\overline{(\Upsilon_n)_s} - H_n\right|>\frac1q\right\} \right) =0,$$
and in particular for any $q \ge 1$ then 
$$
\P\left(\bigcap_{r \ge 1} \bigcup_{s \ge r} \left\{\left|\overline{(\Upsilon_n)_s} - H_n\right|>\frac1q\right\} \right) = \lim_{r \to \infty}\P\left(\bigcup_{s \ge r} \left\{\left|\overline{(\Upsilon_n)_s} - H_n\right|>\frac1q\right\} \right) =0.
$$
Fix $\epsilon > 0$. Since $h_m \ge \lfloor \log_2 m \rfloor$, then $\{|W_m - H_n| > \epsilon\} \subset \bigcup_{s \ge \lfloor \log_2 m \rfloor} \{|\overline{(\Upsilon_n)}_{s} - H_n| > \frac1q\}$ for $\epsilon > 1/q$. It follows 
$$
\lim_{m \to \infty} \P(|W_m - H_n| > \epsilon) \le \lim_{r = \lfloor \log_2 m \rfloor \to \infty} \P\left(\bigcup_{s \ge r} \left\{\left|\overline{(\Upsilon_n)_s} - H_n\right|>\frac1q\right\} \right) = 0.
$$
It follows $W_m \to_p H_n$ as $m \to \infty$.

This handles the lower bounds when $m$ or $n$ is fixed. Now to consider upper bounds, note the maximal height doesn't need to come from a node within a block that has maximal height from the $m$-permutation, as the last can have the minimal height $\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor$, which can be beat in final depth if a node within a $m$-block of depth $h_m + \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor - n$ has maximal $m$ depths this layer (noting the impact for depth is only influenced then by this last layer as no prior layer could then beat this additional depth). Hence
\begin{align*}
    h_{m,n} = \max_{v \in \rho: \operatorname{depth}(v) \ge \lfloor \log_2 m \rfloor + \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor - n}
\end{align*}


Then we can consider more specific wreath product setups. It might be more natural to discuss then butterfly permutations (starting in the other paper) by first going into wreath products. And then work with cases when the product groups are cyclic, where we can further separate then iterated wreath products, along with the simple case (as a wreath product collapses into a Kronecker product when all of the factors are identical so that the semidirect action is necessarily trivial) versus the general case. For cyclic groups, we can further justify starting with the groups we studied, generated using the circulant basis permutation matrices. There is some sort of comparison directly to the Mallows model directly for the circulant model (they focused on the models that biased toward the identity (as a reflection let's them then get the symmetric result for biasing toward the reverse permutation) and ours bias toward longer right-edges (although the), while some additional distinctions are interesting (in our iterated wreath product, then the BST satisfies a fractal structure -- so sub-trees starting at nodes that follow an gluing edge added at an intermediate stage are also butterfly trees, but not for nodes within each block).

