\begin{abstract}
   We prove that over artinian stretched Gorenstein rings, the ideals of minors of a minimal free resolution of any finitely generated module are eventually 2-periodic. This is an analog of results obtained over complete intersections and Golod rings proved by Brown, Dao, and Sridhar.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}

All rings are assumed to be noetherian in this article. Minimal free resolutions of finitely generated modules over a local ring  form a central topic in commutative algebra and algebraic geometry. In general, little is known. It is a classical result by Auslander, Buchsbaum, and Serre that if a ring $R$ is a regular local ring, then every finitely generated module has a finite minimal free resolution. Even over such rings, constructing the minimal free resolution of a cyclic module has become an ever active topic among researchers (e.g., \cite{BPS98}). Over a ring $R$ that is not a regular local ring, most minimal free resolutions are infinite. Homologically, the next rings to look at after regular local rings are hypersurfaces, and indeed one of the most well-known structure results of free resolutions is over such rings. Eisenbud \cite{Ei80} developed the theory of matrix factorizations, with arguably the most remarkable resulting theorem being: over a hypersurface, any minimal free resolution is eventually 2-periodic. We refer to \cite{infinite,MP15} for great surveys in the topic of infinite free resolutions.

Eisenbud's result has inspired researchers to look for periodicity in minimal free resolutions. An explicit description of their differentials in general seems far-fetched still, as it would require, as the minimum, the full understanding of all syzygies of a given module. However, their information can be partially captured by their ideals of minors, also known as \emph{Fitting ideals} \cite{Fit36}, which we shall recall. Let $M$ be a finitely generated over a noetherian local ring $(R,\mathfrak
m)$, and $(\mathcal{F},\partial)$ the minimal free resolution of $M$ over $R$. For given integers $r$ and $n$, let $\bI^r_n(M)$ denote the ideal of $R$ generated by all $r\times r$ minors of the presentation matrix of the differential $\partial_n\colon F_n\to F_{n-1}$. While two minimal free resolutions of $M$ are isomorphic, they are not necessarily the same. However, this does imply that ideals of minors are invariants of the module $M$. Applications of ideals of minors include the celebrated Buchsbaum-Eisenbud's acyclicity criterion \cite{BE73} which states that whether a complex of free $R$-modules is a free resolution depends entirely on the ranks and the corresponding ideals of minors. For more applications on ideals of minors, we refer to \cite{EG94,EH05,Wang94} for a non-exhaustive list. The question of periodicity of ideals of minors is relatively recent, and it has been shown that these ideals are eventually 2-periodic over Golod rings and complete intersections \cite{BDS25}. We shall start with some examples.

\begin{example}\label{ex:CI-periodic}
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Let $R=\Bbbk[y]/(y^3)$ and $M=(y)$. The minimal free resolution of $M$ over $R$ is
        \[
        \cdots \xrightarrow{(y)} R \xrightarrow{(y^2)} R\xrightarrow{(y)} R \xrightarrow{(y^2)}\cdots \xrightarrow{(y)}R\xrightarrow{(y^2)}R\xrightarrow{} 0.
        \]
        \item Let $R=\Bbbk[y,z_1]/(yz_1,y^2-z_1^2)$ and $M=(y)$. The minimal free resolution of $M$ over $R$ is
        \[
        \cdots \xrightarrow{(y)} R\xrightarrow{(z_1)} R \xrightarrow{(y)}R\xrightarrow{(z_1)} \cdots \xrightarrow{(y)}R\xrightarrow{(z_1)}R\xrightarrow{} 0.
        \]
    \end{enumerate}
\end{example}

In both instances of Example~\ref{ex:CI-periodic}, the only non-trivial ideals of minors are the 2-periodic $\bI^1_n(M)$. This phenomenon has been observed and proven in more generality \cite[Theorem 1.2]{BDS25}, as the two rings in the examples are complete intersections. In this article, we extend Example~\ref{ex:CI-periodic} in a different direction: the given two rings are also artinian stretched Gorenstein. An artinian local ring $(R,\frakm)$, given that it is not a field, is called \emph{stretched} if $\frakm^2$ is principal. The theory of stretched Cohen-Macaulay rings was developped by Sally \cite{Sal79}, together with many structure theorems, one specifically for artinian stretched Gorenstein rings which will be used as a main tool in this article. Our main result is an analog of the known results on complete intersections and Golod rings, as follows.


\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
    Let $(R,\frakm, \Bbbk)$ be a homomorphic image of a regular local ring and an artinian stretched Gorenstein ring. Assume that $\characteristic \Bbbk\neq 2$. Then for any finitely generated $R$-module $M$ and any integer $r\geq 1$, we have $\bI^r_n(M)=\bI^r_{n+2}(M)$
    for $n\gg 0$.
\end{theorem}

We briefly discuss the contribution of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, and the methods we will use to prove it. We highlight two aspects in which the result complements the existing literature.

Firstly, the main results of Brown, Dao, and Sridhar \cite{BDS25} were proved using structure theorems of minimal free resolutions over Golod rings and complete intersections. In the case of Golod rings, they used the $A$-infinity structures on minimal free resolutions \cite{Burke15}, and in the case of complete intersections, the key was the theory of higher matrix factorizations \cite{EP16}. Our proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is more elementary, based on explicit constructions of parts of the differentials in minimal free resolutions. In fact, in most of the cases, we shall show that $\bI_n^r(M)=\frakm^r$ for $n\gg0$ (see Proposition~\ref{prop:e>=3}). It is clear that for any integers $r$ and $n$ and any finitely generated $R$-module $M$ where $(R,\frakm)$ is a local ring, we have $\bI^r_n(M)\subseteq \frakm^r$. From this perspective, it simply means that in the right circumstances, $\bI^r_n(M)$ contains $\frakm^r$ for $n\gg 0$, which is a much simpler problem. We note that while the two rings Example~\ref{ex:CI-periodic} are both complete intersections and artinian stretched Gorenstein rings, the ``next" example is not: $\Bbbk[y,z_1,z_2]/(yz_1,yz_2,z_1z_2,y^2-z_1^2,y^2-z_2^2)$ is artinian stretched Gorenstein, and at the same time, neither a complete intersection nor a Golod ring. 

Secondly, another known result on the periodicity of ideals of minors is over rings of Burch index at least 2. For more on Burch index, we refer interested readers to \cite{DE23,DKT20}. Let $(R,\frakm,\Bbbk)$ be a local ring of depth $0$. It is shown in \cite[Theorem 0.1]{DE23} that if $R$ is of Burch index at least 2, then the 7-th syzygy module of any finitely generated non-free module $M$ contains $\Bbbk$ as a direct summand. Coupling this with \cite[Lemma~2.15]{BDS25}, given that $R$ is not a hypersurface, we have that $\bI_n^r(M)=\frakm^r$ for $n\gg 0$. Most rings in our class, artinian stretched Gorenstein rings, are Gorenstein non-hypersurface rings, which are of Burch index 0, and in particular syzygy modules of the residue field $\Bbbk$ do not contain $\Bbbk$ as a direct summand (see \cite[Example 4.5]{DE23}). Nevertheless, as shall be seen in Proposition~\ref{prop:e>=3}, most of the time, we also have that $\bI_n^r(M)=\frakm^r$ for $n\gg 0$.

As a last note, an analog of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} does not hold for artinian Gorenstein rings in general, as has been shown in \cite[Proposition~5.1]{BDS25}.

In Section~\ref{sec:2} we recall some concepts and the process of constructing a minimal free resolution. We prove our main result, Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, in Section~\ref{sec:3}.

